MEASURED at the staged bytes in a throwaway worktree, compiled clean by javac 25, staged, ./scripts/check-io-seam.sh --full exit 0 (`ok 105 business-logic file(s) name none of the 21 banned constructs`):

    // ingest/src/main/java/binjava/ingest/EscA.java
    package binjava.ingest;
    import java.time.LocalDate;
    import java.time.ZoneId;
    final class EscA {
      private final ZoneId zone;
      EscA(ZoneId zone) { this.zone = zone; }
      LocalDate today() { return LocalDate.now(zone); }
    }

EXPLICIT['.now(Zone...)'] is `\.\s*now\s*\(\s*(?:[A-Za-z_$][\w$]*\s*\.\s*)*Zone`, so it fires only when the ARGUMENT's source text begins with the capitalised token `Zone` -- which is true of the sample the case pins (`now(java.time.ZoneOffset.UTC)`) and false of every zone overload called with a variable: `now(zone)`, `now(this.zone)`, `now(zoneId)`, `now(cfg.zone())`. The no-argument shape does not fire either, because the parens are not empty. The entry's own comment claims more than the regex delivers: 'These two and the no-argument form are the JDK's `now` overloads that do NOT take a `Clock`; the one that does is the shape a seam is FOR.' So this is not the enumeration being short -- which the header honestly disclaims and I am not raising -- it is a named entry that does not cover the overload it names, in the exact form (a class holding a ZoneId and asking it for today) that business logic writes when it wants a wall clock. Concrete failure: a scheduler or a retention rule takes a ZoneId seam instead of a Clock seam, reads the host clock through it, passes the gate, and is untestable without moving the machine's time -- the defect non-negotiable 7 exists to stop, under a green line naming 21 constructs. The distinction the regex needs is arity-and-not-a-Clock, not a leading `Zone`: `.now(` with a non-empty argument list is banned unless the argument is a Clock, which is the same shape the `.now()` entry already had to reason about.

MEASURED at the staged bytes in a throwaway worktree. Mutation: insert `'java.io.File',` as the first element of ALLOWED. Then

    // ingest/src/main/java/binjava/ingest/EscE.java
    package binjava.ingest;
    final class EscE { boolean wipe(String path) { return new java.io.File(path).delete(); } }

compiles, stages and passes -- `ok 102 business-logic file(s) name none of the 21 banned constructs`. Under the same mutation the sample that everyBANNEDConstructIsREFUSED and theBYTETypesInABannedPackageAreACCEPTEDWhileItsFILETypesAreNot both pin, `new java.io.PrintStream("/tmp/x")`, is still REFUSED (measured, exit 1), because _allowed_at compares with startswith and 'java.io.PrintStream' does not start with 'java.io.File'. And ALL_ENTRIES = PACKAGES + BANNED, so len() still prints 21 and the coverage assertion at line 408 -- the one mechanism the row claims makes the list self-maintaining -- never sees ALLOWED at all. The accepted-direction cases (URI, URLEncoder, the byte types) only ever get MORE true as ALLOWED grows, so nothing in either test class reds.

That is the same move as round five's escape arriving from the other side: there, java.io was a class-name prefix and PrintStream walked past; here the package is genuinely banned and the hole is the list that punches through it, which is unguarded. It is also the direction non-negotiable 2 forbids -- widening a carve-out weakens the deny-list as surely as deleting an entry -- and the backlog State cell asserts the opposite in terms: 'EVERY ENTRY IS PINNED BY A SAMPLE AND THE TABLE IS CHECKED AGAINST THE LIST ITSELF, so an entry cannot be added without evidence or deleted in silence.' True of BANNED and PACKAGES, false of the newest surface. Concrete failure: a future commit needs one File type for something that looks adapter-ish, adds a line to ALLOWED rather than moving the file to binstore-backends -- which is what the gate's own failure message tells the author to do instead -- and every case stays green with no sample, no measurement and no diff in the printed count. The fix is the mechanism already built: fold ALLOWED into the coverage assertion (its own list, its own sample per entry, each sample ACCEPTED and a near-neighbour REFUSED).
